                            \\begin{aligned}
                            \&amp;
                            \\left\langle
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_1}_{int}(x_1)
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_2}_{int}(x_2)
                            \\cdots
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_{n-1}}_{int}(x_{n-1})
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_n}_{int,sw}(x_n)
                            \\right\rangle
                            \\\
                            \&amp; =
                            \\underset{\underset{g_{sw} \to 1}{\longrightarrow}}{\lim}
                            \\left\langle
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_1}_{int,sw}(x_1)
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_2}_{int,sw}(x_2)
                            \\cdots
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_{n-1}}_{int,sw}(x_{n-1})
                            \\mathbf{\Phi}^{a_n}_{int,sw}(x_n)
                            \\right\rangle
                            \\end{aligned}
                    %p
                      of
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tempered+distribution"} tempered distributional
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
                      of products of
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra"} interacting
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
                      (def.
                      = succeed ")" do
                        %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldObservables"}
                      = succeed ".)" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
                      = succeed "." do
                        %em
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+adiabatic+limit"} weak adiabatic limit
                %p
                  Beware that the left hand sides here are symbolic: Even if the limit exists in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
                %p
                  The strong and weak adiabatic limits have been shown to exist if all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                  are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} massive
                  (
                  %a{:href => "S-matrix#EpsteinGlaser73"}> Epstein-Glaser 73
                  ). The weak adiabatic limit has been shown to exists for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
                  and for
                  = succeed "-less" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
                  (
                  %a{:href => "adiabatic+switching#BlanchardSeneor75"}> Blanchard-Seneor 75
                  ) and for larger classes of field theories in (
                  %a{:href => "adiabatic+switching#Duch17"}> Duch 17, p. 113, 114
                  ).
                %p
                  If these limits do not exist, one says that the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
                  has an
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergence"} infrared divergence
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"}> algebraic adiabatic limit
                  ) On the other hand, it is equally unrealistic that an actual
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
                  %em detects
                  phenomena outside a given compact subset of spacetime. Realistic scattering
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
                  (such as the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
                  filling all of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                  as described by the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ScatteringPlaneWaves"} (10)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                  that is realistically measurable must have compact spacetime support. We see below in prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IsomorphismFromChangeOfAdiabaticSwitching"}
                  that such
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+observables"} interacting field observables
                  with compact spacetime support may be computed without taking the
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+limit"} adiabatic limit
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
                  which is constant on the support of the observable.
                %p
                  This way one obtains for each
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+closed+subset"} causally closed subset
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
                  of spacetime an algebra of observables
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi int
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_{int}(\mathcal{O})
                  whose support is in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}
                  below). Of this system of observables one may form the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+limit"} inductive limit
                  to obtain a single global algebra of observables.
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi int
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ≔
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mi lim
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi 𝒪
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi int
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathcal{A}_{int}
                        \\;\coloneqq\;
                        \\underset{\underset{\mathcal{O}}{\longrightarrow}}{\lim}
                        \\mathcal{A}_{int}(\mathcal{O})
                %p
                  This always exists. It is called the
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
                  (going back to
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00, section 8
                %p
                  For
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
                  was worked out in (
                  %a{:href => "quantum+electrodynamics#DuetschFredenhagen98"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 98
                  , reviewed in
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "QED+Duetsch18"} Dütsch 18, 5,3
              %li
                %p
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+vacuum"}> interacting vacuum
                  ) While, via the above
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                  yields the correct
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra+of+quantum+observables"} interacting field algebra of quantum observables
                  independent of choices of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability"} quantum probability
                  requires, on top of the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+on+a+star-algebra"} state
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mi int
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi int
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\langle - \rangle_{int} \;\colon\; \mathcal{A}_{int} \longrightarrow \mathbb{C}[ [\hbar] ]
                %p
                  Just as the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra+of+observables"} interacting field algebra of observables
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mi int
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_{int}
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation"} deformation
                  of the free field algebra of observables (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"}> Wick algebra
                  ), there ought to be a corresponding deformation of the free
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+vacuum+state"} Hadamard vacuum state
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle- \rangle
                  into an “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+vacuum+state"}> interacting vacuum state
                  ”
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mi int
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - \rangle_{int}
                %p
                  Sometimes the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+adiabatic+limit"} weak adiabatic limit
                  serves to define the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+vacuum"} interacting vacuum
                  (see
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "adiabatic+switching#Duch17"} Duch 17, p. 113-114
            %p
              A stark example of these infrared issues is the phenomenon of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
              (notably to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavelengths"} wavelengths
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} observation
              and reproduced in numerical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
              simulation, but is invisible to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
              in its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"} infrared divergences
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+vacuum"} interacting vacuum
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              (
              %a{:href => "confinement#Rafelski90"}> Rafelski 90, pages 12-16
              ), which is possibly a “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta-vacuum"}> theta-vacuum
              ” exhibiting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+in+QCD"} QCD instantons
              (
              %a{:href => "instanton+in+QCD#SchaeferShuryak98"}> Schäfer-Shuryak 98, section III.D
              ). This remains open, closely related to the
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/millennium-problems/yang%E2%80%93mills-and-mass-gap"} Millennium Problem
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            In contrast to the above subtleties about the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"} infrared divergences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            are dealt with by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
          #TheTraditionalErrorThatLeadsToTheNotoriouDivergencies.num_remark
            %h6#remark_10 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (the traditional error leading to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            %p
              Naively it might seem that (say over
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProduct"}
              might simply be obtained by multiplying
              = succeed "-products" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/step+functions"} step functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
              of the time coordinates, hence to write, in the notation as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
              (remark
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NotationForTimeOrderedProductsAsGeneralizedFunctions"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo =
                      %mtext no!
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T(x_1, x_2)
                    \\overset{\text{no!}}{=}
                    \\Theta(x_1^0 - x_2^0) \, T(x_1) \, T(x_2)
                    +
                    \\Theta(x_2^0 - x_1^0) \, T(x_2) \, T(x_1)
            %p
              and analogously for time-ordered products of more arguments (for instance
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#Weinberg95"} Weinberg 95, p. 143, between (3.5.9) and (3.5.10)
            %p
              This however is simply a mathematical error (as amplified in
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "causal+perturbation+theory#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, below (3.2.4), below (3.2.44) and in fig. 3
            %p
              Both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              as well as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
              and their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
              is in general not defined, as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"} Hörmander's criterion
              (prop.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HoermanderCriterionForProductOfDistributions"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
              (remark
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UltravioletDivergencesFromPaleyWiener"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergences"} ultraviolet divergences
              which plagued (
              %a{:href => "Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson#Feynman85SuchABunchOfWords"}> Feynman 85
              ) the original conception of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              due to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
              are the signature of this ill-defined product (see remark
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalPerturbationTheoryAbsenceOfUVDivergences"}
            %p
              On the other hand, when both distributions are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction+of+distributions"} restricted
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
              (i.e. restricted away from coinciding points
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1 = x_2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/step+function"} step function
              becomes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-singular+distribution"} non-singular distribution
              so that the above expression happens to be well defined and does solve the axioms for time-ordered products.
            %p
              Hence what needs to be done to properly define the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
              is to choose an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
              of the above product expression back from the complement of the diagonal to the whole space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
              of points. Any such extension will produce time-ordered products.
            %p
              There are in general several different such
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extensions
              = succeed ";" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} "re-"normalization
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization+theory"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization theory
              (theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeRenormalizationMainTheorem"}
              below), this is the freedom of choosing “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"}> counterterms
              ” (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TermCounter"}
              below) for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
              %a{:href => "#ExistenceAndRenormalization"} below
              and in more detail in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Renormalization"} next chapter
          #CausalPerturbationTheoryAbsenceOfUVDivergences.num_remark
            %h6#remark_11 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (absence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergences"} ultraviolet divergences
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} re-normalization
            %p
              The simple axioms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              “in the ultraviolet”: A solution to these axioms induces, by definition, well-defined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              (remark
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FromAxiomaticSMatrixScatteringAmplitudes"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+observables"} interacting field observables
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldObservables"}
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
              (describing point-interactions such as the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization
              (theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeRenormalizationMainTheorem"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              (remark
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AsymptoticSeriesObservables"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              of these formal power series (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"} loop order
              contributions”) are well defined.
            %p
              This is in contrast to the original informal conception of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              due to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} diverging
              expressions for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              (due to the mathematical error discussed in remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheTraditionalErrorThatLeadsToTheNotoriouDivergencies"}
              below), which were then “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> re-normalized
              ” to finite values, by further informal arguments.
            %p
              Here in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
              no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergences"} divergences
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              are considered in the first place, all coefficients are well-defined, hence “finite”. In this sense
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
              is about “finite” perturbative QFT, where instead of “re-normalization” of ill-defined expressions one just encounters “normalization” (prominently highlighted in
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "causal+perturbation+theoryscatt#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, see title, introduction, and section 4.3
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
            %p
              This refers to those
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergences"} divergences
              that are known as
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              scheme (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdiabaticLimit"}
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"} infrared divergences
          #WorldlineFormalism.num_remark
            %h6#remark_12 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"}> virtual particles
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %p
              It is suggestive to think of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagram"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
              of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"}> virtual particles
              ” and of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              as the points where they collide and transmute. (Care must be exercised not to confuse this with concepts of real
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagramAmplitude"}
              may be read as saying that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
              for given external
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source+fields"} source fields
              (remark
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FromAxiomaticSMatrixScatteringAmplitudes"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
              of all possible ways that these may interact; which is closely related to the intuition for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
              (remark
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InterpretationOfPerturbativeSMatrix"}
            %p
              This intuition is made precise by the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "worldline+formalism#Strassler92"}> Strassler 92
              ). This is the perspective on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              which directly relates
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
              (
              %a{:href => "worldline+formalism#SchmidtSchubert94"}> Schmidt-Schubert 94
              ). In fact the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              was originally found by taking thre point-particle limit of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
              (
              %a{:href => "worldline+formalism#BernKosower91"}> Bern-Kosower 91
              ,
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "worldline+formalism#BernKosower92"} Bern-Kosower 92
          #calSFunctionIsRenormalizationScheme.num_remark
            %h6#remark_13 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"}> renormalization scheme
                )
            %p
              Beware the terminology in def.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              %em single
              S-matrix is one single observable
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi PolyObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi mc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{S}(S_{int})
                    \\;\in\;
                    PolyObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})_{mc}((\hbar))[ [g,j] ]
            %p
              for a fixed (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{int}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              (remark
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FromAxiomaticSMatrixScatteringAmplitudes"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi LocObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi PolyObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{S}
                    \\;\colon\;
                    LocObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})[ [\hbar, g,j] ]\langle g, j
                    \\rangle
                    \\longrightarrow
                    PolyObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})((\hbar))[ [g,j] ]
            %p
              axiomatized in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              is really a whole
              %em scheme
              for constructing compatible S-matrices for
              %em all
              possible (adiabatically switched, local) interactions at once.
            %p
              Since the usual proof of the construction of such schemes of S-matrices involves
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              axiomatized by def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              may also be referred to as a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} ("re"-)normalization scheme
            %p
              This perspective on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} renormalization scheme
              is amplified by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization
              (theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeRenormalizationMainTheorem"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
              over the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_14 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"}> quantum anomalies
                )
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              (and similarly that for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              below in def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProduct"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+net"} causally local net
              of perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebras+of+quantum+observables"} interacting field algebras of quantum observables
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}
              below), and thus its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
              (remark
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdiabaticLimit"}
            %p
              It does not guarantee, however, that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+differential"} BV-BRST differential
              passes to those
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries+of+the+Lagrangian"} infinitesimal symmetries of the Lagrangian
              are respected by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              process (there may be “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"}> quantum anomalies
              ”). The extra condition that does ensure this is the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+Ward+identity"} quantum master Ward identity
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            %p
              Apart from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetries"} gauge symmetries
              one also wants to require that rigid symmetries are preserved by the S-matrix, notably
              = succeed "-symmetry" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#LocalNetsOfInteractingFieldObservables
            %strong Interacting field observables
          %p
            We now discuss how the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+observables"} interacting field observables
            which are induced from an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            enjoy good properties expected of any abstractly defined
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          #QuntumMollerOperator.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra+of+observables"} interacting field algebra of observables
                –
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+M%C3%B8ller+operator"} quantum Møller operator
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              according to def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VacuumFree"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              be a corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              scheme according to def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi LocObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g S_{int} \in LocObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})[ [ \hbar, g ] ]
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local observable
              regarded as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} functional
            %p We write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi LocIntObs
                      %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi int
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi LocObs
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mrow
                          %mi BV
                          %mo −
                          %mi BRST
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
