                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \begin{aligned} &amp; A_1 \star_F A_2 = \\ &amp; ((-)\cdot (-)) \circ \exp \left( \hbar \int (\Delta_F)^{ab}(x,y) \frac{\delta}{\delta \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)} \otimes \frac{\delta}{\delta \mathbf{\Phi}^b(y)} \right)(A_1 \otimes A_2) \end{aligned}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#FeynmanDiagramInTwoStages
            We now discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            in two stages: First we consider plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linearly ordered
            vertices but no other labels (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
            below) and discuss how these generally organize an expansion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            as a sum of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products+of+distributions"} distributional products
            of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
            below). These summands (or their
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            if one thinks of the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            as having a single “field species” and of the chosen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            to be a single “interaction vertex”.
          %p
            But often it is possible and useful to identify different field species and different interaction vertices. In fact in applications this choice is typically evident and not highlighted as a choice. We make it explicit below as def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VerticesAndFieldSpecies"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term as well as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            decompose as sums (remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPropagatorFieldSpecies"}
            below). Accordingly then, after “multiplying out” the products of these sums that appear in the Feynman amplitudes, these, too, decompose further as as sums indexed by multigraphs whose edges are labeled by field species, and whose vertices are labeled by interactions. These labeled multigraphs are the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagram"}
            below) and the corresponding summands are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            proper (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagramAmplitude"}
            below).
