                    \\Delta(x,-)^{\beta, -}
                    }
                    }
            %p
              Hence a typical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
              induced by this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
              looks as follows:
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramGlobal.jpg", :width => "560"}/
            %p
              where on the bottom the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
              is shown; now notationally suppressing the contraction of the internal indices and all prefactors.
            %p
              For instance the two solid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_3
              correspond to the two factors of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(x_2,x_2)
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponent1.jpg", :width => "560"}/
            %p
              This way each sub-graph encodes its corresponding subset of factors in the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentTwo.jpg", :width => "560"}/
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentThree.jpg", :width => "560"}/
            %blockquote
              %p
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "Feynman+diagram#Brouder10"} Brouder 10
            %p
              A priori this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
              is defined away from coincident vertices:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≠
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \neq x_j
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"}
              below). The definition at coincident vertices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i = x_j
              requires a choice of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfDistributions"}
              below) to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
              locus of coincident interaction points. This choice is the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re-")normalization
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfTimeOrderedProoductsRenormalization"}
              below) of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
          #FeynmanPerturbationSeries.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"}> Feynman perturbation series
                )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              according to def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VacuumFree"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi LocObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g S_{int} + j A
                    \\;\in\;
                    LocObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})[ [ \hbar, g, h ] ]\langle g , j\rangle
            %p
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local observable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %p
              By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
              every choice of perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi PolyObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi mc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{S}(g S_{int} + j A)
                    \\;\in\;
                    PolyObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})_{mc}((\hbar))[ [ g, j ] ] +
            %p
              has an expansion as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒢
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                      %mo +
                      %mi j
                      %mi A
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi ν
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{S}(g S_{int} + j A)
                    \\;=\;
                    \\underset{\Gamma \in \mathcal{G}}{\sum}
                    \\Gamma\left( (g S_{int} + j A)_{i = 1}^{\nu(\Gamma)}\right)
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the series is over all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linearly ordered
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              (def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalized
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfTimeOrderedProoductsRenormalization"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
              (prop.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
            %p
              If moreover a choice of field species and of internal and external interaction vertices is made, according to def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VerticesAndFieldSpecies"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi edgelab
                    %mo ,
                    %mi vertlab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma,edgelab, vertlab)
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagram"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi edgelab
                    %mo ,
                    %mi vertlab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma, edgelab,vertlab)(g S_{int} + j A)
              (def.
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagramAmplitude"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi edgelab
                        %mo ,
                        %mi vertlab
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mi Feyn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi edgelab
                    %mo ,
                    %mi vertlab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{S}(g S_{int} + j A)
                    \\;=\;
                    \\underset{(\Gamma,edgelab, vertlab) \in \mathcal{G}^{Feyn}}{\sum}
                    (\Gamma, edgelab,vertlab)(g S_{int} + j A)
                    \\,,
            %p
              Expressed in this form the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              is known as the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
          #Tadpoles.num_remark
            %h6#remark_17 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (no
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tadpole"} tadpole
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %p
              In the definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
              in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
              there are
              %em no
              edges considered that go from any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertex
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %em itself
              %em no
              such labeled edges in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
              (def.
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagram"}
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/tadpole.png", :width => "70"}/
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pQFT"} pQFT
              these diagrams are called
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tadpoles"} tadpoles
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
              (prop.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
              this condition reflects the nature of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PropagatorStarProduct"}
              %em different
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              factors with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
              before taking their pointwise product.
            %p
              Beware that in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
              these
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tadpoles"} tadpoles
              are called “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loops"}> loops
              ”, while here in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pQFT"} pQFT
              a “loop” in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+graph"} planar graph
              refers instead to what in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
              is called a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/face"} face
              of the graph, see the discussion of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"} loop order
              in prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagramLoopOrder"}
              below.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#Keller10"}> Keller 10, remark II.8 and proof of prop. II.7
            )
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#EffectiveAction
            %strong Effective action
          %p
            We have seen that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            expresses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            labeled by
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
            associated with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+graph"} connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConnectedGraphs"}
            below) is just the product of the amplitudes of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LogarithmEffectiveAction"}
            below). This allows to re-organize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            as the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
            of the Feynman perturbation series restricted to just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+graph"} connected
            Feynman diagrams. The latter is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
            below) because it allows to express
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            as an ordinary exponential (equation
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ExponentialSeffVEVOfSMatrix"} (23)
            below).
          #ConnectedGraphs.num_defn
            %h6#definition_21 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+graphs"}> connected graphs
                )
            %p
              Given two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_1, \Gamma_2 \in \mathcal{G}
              (def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma_1 \sqcup \Gamma_2
                    \\;\in\;
                    \\mathcal{G}
            %p
              is the finite multigraph whose set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              and set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+unions"} disjoint unions
              of the corresponding sets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊔
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V_{\Gamma_1 \sqcup \Gamma_2}
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    V_{\Gamma_1} \sqcup V_{\Gamma_2}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊔
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    E_{\Gamma_1 \sqcup \Gamma_2}
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    E_{\Gamma_1} \sqcup E_{\Gamma_2}
            %p
              and whose vertex-assigning function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is the corresponding function on disjoint unions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊔
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p_{\Gamma_1 \sqcup \Gamma_2}
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    p_{\Gamma_1} \sqcup p_{\Gamma_2}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The operation induces a pairing on the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo ×
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \sqcup (-)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{G}
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathcal{G}
                    \\,.
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraph"} finite multigraph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \in \mathcal{G}
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+graph"} connected
              if it is not the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
              of two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited"} non-empty
              finite multigraphs.
            %p We write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mi conn
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_{conn}
                    \\subset
                    \\mathcal{G}
            %p
              for the subset of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+graph"} connected
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
          #MultiplicativeFeynmanAmplitudes.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
                multiply under
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
            %p Let
            .maruku-equation
