          #TerminologyForEffectiveAction.num_remark
            %h6#remark_18 Remark
            %p
              %strong (terminology for “effective action”)
            %p Beware differing conventions of terminology:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  In the perspective of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
                  (remark
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#pQFTEffective"}
                  below), the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                  in def.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                  is sometimes called the
                  %em effective potential
                  at scale
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda = 0
                  (see prop.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                  below).
                %p
                  This terminology originates in restriction to the special example of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
                  (example
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RealScalarFieldBundle"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phi%5En+interactions"} Phi^n interactions
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi int
                        %mo =
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi n
                        %munder
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi Σ
                        %msubsup
                          %mi g
                          %mi sw
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Φ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi n
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi dvol
                          %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g S_{int} = \underset{n}{\sum} \underset{\Sigma}{\int} g_{sw}^{(n)}(x) (\mathbf{\Phi}(x))^n \, dvol_\Sigma(x)
                  (example
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#phintheoryLagrangian"}
                  = succeed "-terms." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential energy
                %p
                  From this perspective the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                  in def.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                  is a special case of
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+actions"} relative effective actions
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mi eff
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{eff,\Lambda}
                  (“relative effective potentials”, in the case of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phi%5En+interactions"} Phi^n interactions
                  = succeed "-scales" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                  (def.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EffectiveActionRelative"}
                  below).
              %li
                %p For the special case that
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mi A
                        %mo ≔
                        %munder
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi Σ
                        %msub
                          %mi j
                          %mrow
                            %mi sw
                            %mo ,
                            %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Φ
                          %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
